4.  Status:

    - This document is a DRAFT repository-level containerization specification.
    - This document is not an Internet Standards Track specification.
    - This document MUST not be used in production without proper testing.

5.  Language:

    - "Service" refers to containerized application component.
    - "Container" refers to Docker container instance.
    - "Compose" refers to Docker Compose orchestration.
    - "Proxy" refers to nginx reverse proxy configuration.
    - "Volume" refers to Docker volume mount for persistent data.

6.  Conventions:

    - PowerShell scripts MUST use .ps1 file extension.
    - Shell scripts MUST use .sh file extension.
    - Docker files MUST use Dockerfile prefix with service name.
    - Service names MUST use kebab-case format.
    - Port numbers MUST match original task configurations.

7.  Purpose:

    - Containerize all core services for cross-platform deployment.
    - Maintain development experience consistency across environments.
    - Replace caddy with nginx for standardized reverse proxy.
    - Provide lightweight Alpine-based container images.
    - Enable hot reload and file watching for development workflow.

8.  Structure:

    - Prerequisites:
        - Docker Engine 20.10 or later MUST be installed.
        - Docker Compose 2.0 or later MUST be installed.
        - PowerShell 7.5.5 or later MUST be installed for Windows.
        - Bash shell MUST be available for Linux/macOS.
        - Ports 3690, 4979, 5173, 6039, 9630 MUST be available.
        - Minimum 4GB RAM MUST be available for containers.
        - Minimum 2GB disk space MUST be available for images.
        - Git MUST be installed for source code access.
        - Internet connection MUST be available for image downloads.

    - Docker Compose orchestrates 5 containerized services.
    - Storybook service runs on port 3690 for component development.
    - Images File Service runs on port 5173 serving static assets.
    - Products Data Service runs on port 4979 as Go/Fiber API.
    - Products Component Service runs on port 6039 as Next.js service.
    - Prototype Service runs on port 9630 as main nginx reverse proxy.

    - Service Dependencies:
        - Prototype Service depends on all other services.
        - Products Component Service depends on Data and Images services.
        - All services connect via core-network bridge network.

    - Volume Mounts:
        - Source code mounted for live development.
        - Node modules cached in anonymous volumes.
        - Products cache persisted in named volume.
        - Nginx configurations mounted read-only.

    - Environment Variables:
        - IMAGE_ASSETS_DIRECTORY_PATH for image assets location.
        - PRODUCTS_CACHE_PATH for products database location.
        - PRODUCT_DATA_SERVICE_ADDRESS for API service URL.
        - IMAGES_FILE_SERVICE_ADDRESS for static assets URL.

    - Health Checks:
        - HTTP health endpoints for service monitoring.
        - 30-second interval with 10-second timeout.
        - 3 retry attempts before marking unhealthy.

    - Management Scripts:
        - start-services.ps1 for Windows PowerShell execution.
        - start-services.sh for Linux/macOS bash execution.
        - stop-services.sh for Linux/macOS service termination.
        - Consolidated script handles validation, diagnostics, and fixes.
        - No separate validation or troubleshooting scripts needed.
        - Automatic elevation and Docker issue resolution.

    - Docker Configuration:
        - docker-compose.yml orchestrates all services.
        - dockerfiles/ contains individual service Dockerfiles.
        - .dockerignore optimizes build context.
        - Alpine Linux base images for minimal footprint.
        - Modular nginx configuration using default.conf files.
        - Nginx configurations mounted as volumes for live updates.
        - Base nginx image provides global configuration defaults.

9.  Flow:

    - Containerized Approach:

        - Starting Services:
            - Navigate to containers/core/prototype-service directory.
            - Windows: Execute ".\start-services.ps1" in PowerShell.
            - Linux/macOS: Execute "./start-services.sh" in terminal.
            - Script automatically validates prerequisites and fixes issues.
            - Script elevates to Administrator privileges when needed.
            - Script ensures Docker is working and starts all services.
            - Wait for all services to report healthy status.
            - Access main application at http://localhost:9630.

        - Stopping Services:
            - Navigate to containers/core/prototype-service directory.
            - Linux/macOS: Execute "./stop-services.sh" in terminal.
            - Windows: Execute "docker-compose down" directly.
            - Verify all containers are stopped and removed.

        - Service Management:
            - View logs: "docker-compose logs -f [service-name]".
            - Restart service: "docker-compose restart [service-name]".
            - Rebuild service: "docker-compose up --build [service-name]".
            - Check health: Access service health endpoints directly.
            - Monitor status: "docker-compose ps" shows service status.

        - Development Workflow:
            - Services automatically detect source code changes.
            - Storybook and Next.js services support hot reload.
            - Go service requires manual restart for code changes.
            - Nginx configurations reload automatically via volume mounts.
            - Modular default.conf files enable live configuration updates.
            - Volume mounts enable live development without rebuilds.

    - Manual Approach:

        - Prerequisites:

            - Windows (PowerShell 7+):
                - winget install -e --id Microsoft.PowerShell --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements && winget install -e --id Volta.Volta --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements && winget install -e --id GoLang.Go --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements && winget install -e --id Nginx.Nginx --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements && winget install -e --id Git.Git --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements
                - Restart PowerShell, then run: volta install node@24.14.0 npm@11.11.0

            - macOS (Homebrew):
                - brew install volta go nginx git && volta install node@24.14.0 npm@11.11.0

            - Linux (Debian/Ubuntu):
                - sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y golang-go git nginx curl && curl -fsSL https://get.volta.sh | bash && export VOLTA_HOME="$HOME/.volta" && export PATH="$VOLTA_HOME/bin:$PATH" && volta install node@24.14.0 npm@11.11.0

        - Start services in separate terminals (order matters):

            - Windows (PowerShell 7+), from repo root:

                - $REPO = (Get-Location).Path

                - Storybook (3690):
                    - cd prototype\web\core; npm ci && npm run storybook

                - Images File Service (5173) via nginx:
                    - $CFG="$env:TEMP\vs-images.conf"; $PUB="$REPO\prototype\web\core\public"; (Get-Content .\services\core\images-file-service\nginx\default.conf) -replace '/usr/share/nginx/html', $PUB | Set-Content $CFG; nginx -c $CFG -g "daemon off;"

                - Products Data Service (4979):
                    - $env:PRODUCTS_CACHE_PATH="$REPO\services\core\products-data-service\fiber-go\data\products-cache.db"; cd services\core\products-data-service\fiber-go; go mod tidy && go run main.go

                - Products Component Service (6039):
                    - $env:PRODUCT_DATA_SERVICE_ADDRESS="http://localhost:4979"; $env:IMAGES_FILE_SERVICE_ADDRESS="http://localhost:5173"; cd services\core\products-component-service\nextjs; npm ci && npm run dev

                - Prototype Service (9630) via nginx:
                    - $CFG2="$env:TEMP\vs-proxy.conf"; (Get-Content .\services\core\prototype-service\nginx\default.conf) -replace 'server storybook:3690;','server 127.0.0.1:3690;' -replace 'server images-file-service:5173;','server 127.0.0.1:5173;' -replace 'server products-data-service:4979;','server 127.0.0.1:4979;' -replace 'server products-component-service:6039;','server 127.0.0.1:6039;' | Set-Content $CFG2; nginx -c $CFG2 -g "daemon off;"

            - macOS/Linux (bash), from repo root:

                - REPO="$(pwd)"

                - Storybook (3690):
                    - cd prototype/web/core && npm ci && npm run storybook

                - Images File Service (5173) via nginx:
                    - CFG="$(mktemp)"; sed "s|/usr/share/nginx/html|$REPO/prototype/web/core/public|g" services/core/images-file-service/nginx/default.conf > "$CFG"; nginx -c "$CFG" -g "daemon off;"

                - Products Data Service (4979):
                    - export PRODUCTS_CACHE_PATH="$REPO/services/core/products-data-service/fiber-go/data/products-cache.db"; cd services/core/products-data-service/fiber-go && go mod tidy && go run main.go

                - Products Component Service (6039):
                    - export PRODUCT_DATA_SERVICE_ADDRESS="http://localhost:4979" IMAGES_FILE_SERVICE_ADDRESS="http://localhost:5173"; cd services/core/products-component-service/nextjs && npm ci && npm run dev

                - Prototype Service (9630) via nginx:
                    - CFG2="$(mktemp)"; sed -e 's|server storybook:3690;|server 127.0.0.1:3690;|' -e 's|server images-file-service:5173;|server 127.0.0.1:5173;|' -e 's|server products-data-service:4979;|server 127.0.0.1:4979;|' -e 's|server products-component-service:6039;|server 127.0.0.1:6039;|' services/core/prototype-service/nginx/default.conf > "$CFG2"; nginx -c "$CFG2" -g "daemon off;"

        - Stop services:
            - Press Ctrl+C in each terminal.
            - Confirm ports 3690, 4979, 5173, 6039, 9630 are free.

A.  Compliance:

    - Agents MUST use PowerShell 7.5.5 or later for .ps1 scripts.
    - Agents MUST ensure Docker and Docker Compose are installed.
    - Agents MUST run start-services.ps1 which handles all requirements.
    - Agents MUST allow elevation to Administrator when prompted.
    - Agents MUST follow the automated service startup sequence.
    - Agents MUST NOT modify core service configurations without approval.

